      "effect": "costs",
      "direction": null,
      "quote": "The limit on residential rent increases is repealed.",
      "mechanism": "Removes existing protections that cap how much a renter's housing costs can increase in a given year.",
      "certainty": "stated"
    },
    {
      "id": "f2",
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "concern_id": "C15",
      "effect": "advances",
      "direction": null,
      "quote": "A landlord may raise rent by any amount",
      "mechanism": "Expands property owners' control over the income generated by their residential units by removing pricing restrictions.",
      "certainty": "stated"
    },
    {
      "id": "f3",
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "concern_id": "C07",
      "effect": "costs",
      "direction": null,
      "quote": "The limit on residential rent increases is repealed. A landlord may raise rent by any amount",
      "mechanism": "Removes a government cap on a major household charge, exposing tenants to potentially unlimited price increases.",
      "certainty": "stated"
    }
  ],
  "analysis": [
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "answer": "Current law prohibits residential landlords from raising rent by more than 5 percent in a 12-month period, unless the unit is less than 15 years old. The measure repeals this limit entirely and allows unlimited rent increases with 60 days' notice.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "answer": "Landlords and property owners benefit by gaining the ability to set rents at market rates or higher to maximize returns. Renters pay the cost by facing steeper rent hikes and potential displacement if they cannot afford the new rates.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "answer": "Landlords in high-demand markets are likely to increase rents beyond the previous 5% cap. Tenants may be forced to pay a higher share of their income toward rent, relocate, or face eviction. In the longer term, the removal of rent caps could alter developers' calculations, potentially incentivizing new residential investment or property upgrades that were previously constrained by limited revenue growth.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "answer": "The measure addresses the problem of property owners being unable to adjust rents to match market rates, inflation, or rising maintenance costs. However, in doing so, it exposes tenants to the problem of sudden rent shocks and unequal bargaining power in tight housing markets.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "answer": "Landlords gain total discretion over residential rent pricing.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q7",
      "answer": "The effects of rent control and its repeal are empirically disputed. Economic research often finds that rent control reduces housing supply, quality, and tenant mobility over the long term, suggesting repeal spurs investment. Conversely, housing and public health research finds that repealing rent limits causes immediate displacement, rent shocks, and financial instability for current vulnerable renters.",
      "disputed": true
    }
  ],
